# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever clones.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout. That is invisible until something parses one: the Windows CI job fetched 0 of
# 207 corpus files because every URL it built carried a trailing carriage return from the
# manifest's last field, and printed them looking truncated, because a terminal draws what
# follows a \r over the start of the line.
#
# So: anything a shell reads — as a script, or as data — is LF on every platform.

*.sh          text eol=lf
*.manifest    text eol=lf
blix          text eol=lf

# The Windows front door is the one file that wants the other ending. cmd.exe copes with
# LF, but a batch file is CRLF by convention and nothing here benefits from being unusual.
*.cmd         text eol=crlf

# Shaders are read by glslc and by the tune scanner. Neither minds, and a diff that flips
# every line because someone's editor did is worse than either ending.
*.glsl        text eol=lf
*.vert        text eol=lf
*.frag        text eol=lf
*.comp        text eol=lf
